 e News/John Buckleyl @angor) \nBiII Buckley @angor) \nI. Summarry of the C
 ase: \nMichelle Geaghan alteged that Respondents discriminated against her
  based on her age and/or sex by \nterminating her employment. She firther 
 alleged that Respondents retaliated against her after she filed \nher Comp
 laint with the Maine Human Rights Commission ("Commission") by voiding her
  severance \nagreement. Respondents denied discrimination and retaliation\
 , positing that it laid her offdue to \nfinancial reasons and could not ha
 ve retaliated against her because she was no longer an employee. \nThe Inv
 estigator conducted a preliminary investigation\, which included reviewing
  the documents \nsubmitted by the parties\, holding an Issues and Resoluti
 ons Conference ("RC")\, and requesting \ninformation from the parties. Bas
 ed upon this information\, the lnvestigator recommends a finding that \nth
 ere are no reasonable grounds to believe that Respondents discriminated ag
 ainst Complainant based \non her age and/or sex. The Investigator recommen
 ds a finding that there are reasonable grounds to \nbelieve that Responden
 ts retaliated against Complainant based on her Complaint. \nII. Jurisdicti
 onal Data: \n1) Date(s) of alleged discrimination: September 24\,2018 - De
 cember 4\,2018 \n2) Date complaint filed with the Commission: October 29\,
 2018 \n3) Respondent has four employees and is subject to the Maine Human 
 Rights Act (*MHRA")\, as well \nas state and employment regulations. \n4) 
 Complainant is not represented by counsel. Respondent is represented by Ry
 an Dumais\, Esq. \nl Complainant named Coffee News//John Buckle\nhttps://w
 ww.maine.gov/mhrc/sites/mhrc/files/inline-files/E18-0420-AB_r.pdf
